  "selection": "Four joint-action candidates cross mixed/local features with .1/.25 turn corrections; cruising speeds and value/credit settings remain frozen. Each candidate has matched globally frozen, memory-disabled, turn-disabled and binary speed-only controls, plus shared existing-neural and incumbent controls. Prefer cue-component retention, nonnegative mean population differences against every development control in BOTH worlds, stable mean energy >= -0.5 versus incumbent, and positive relocation mean energy versus incumbent, turnOff and speedOnly. Rank by worst mean energy across frozen/noMemory/neural in both worlds plus relocating incumbent/turnOff/speedOnly. Tie by ID. Lock once before untouched seeds; evaluate an ineligible best candidate only as exploratory, without reselection.",
  "interpretation": "Human-designed action-interface experiment, not RSI or proof that speed-only control caused earlier failures. Four options combine slow/fast movement with left/right corrections. One memory holds 17 sensory coordinates plus a selected-speed ACTION tag; the stored binary action is the selected turn-correction sign. Motor clipping is recorded separately. Each decision/next-value query charges two full memory scans. Same founders, sensors, topology, physical prices and total record bound; reproduction/mutation off and no environment stage increase. Both neural motor updates are disabled to avoid assigning credit to unexecuted neural samples. Joint model/checkpoint schema differs from binary mode. Gate requires original four controls in BOTH worlds, incumbent preservation/improvement, and stable noninferiority plus positive relocation energy lower bound with nonnegative population mean versus turnOff and speedOnly. The separate four-choice cue task rewards correct turns with freely selected speed tags; it tests retrieval retention, not habitat coordination or whole-body continual learning.",
